          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Machines</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these machines. They were chosen from published specifications,
            maker product pages and FAQs, and independent reviews, and the claims on this page are
            limited to what those sources support.
          </p>
          <p>
            We started from the five ways people actually add cables to a home gym: a freestanding
            functional trainer with two weight stacks, a cable attachment that bolts into an
            existing rack, a plate-loaded pulley tower, a compact all-in-one machine that combines
            cables with rack uprights, and a connected machine that uses motors instead of iron.
            For each type we looked for a current model from a maker that publishes a full
            specification, then checked that it was on sale under that name on 16 October 2026.
          </p>
          <p>
            We then compared the stack weight, pulley ratio, footprint, height and price on the
            maker's page with at least one independent source — Garage Gym Reviews for the REP and
            Bells of Steel machines and the <a href="/best-smart-home-gyms/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Tonal</a> 2, and PCWorld for the Speediance. Where the two
            agreed, the figure went on the card. Where they did not, we left it out: the overall
