            36 by a reviewer, REP's own Athena page gives two different figures for the depth it
            adds to a rack, and the Tonal 2's wall depth differs by a quarter of an inch between
            Tonal and a reviewer. None of those gaps is large, but we would rather tell you to
            check than pick one.
          </p>
          <p>
            We checked every brand on this page against the US Consumer Product Safety
            Commission's recall database on 16 October 2026. We found no recalls for Bells of
            Steel, Tonal or Speediance. REP Fitness had a 2021 recall alert for the strap safety
            brackets sold for its PR-4000 and PR-5000 racks — not for any cable attachment, but
            worth knowing because the Athena mounts to those racks; if you are buying a used REP
            rack, check which brackets came with it. The database also lists a December 2024
            recall of Precor's Resolute cable multi-station, a commercial machine not on this page,
            which is relevant if you are shopping for used commercial cable equipment.
          </p>
          <p>
            We excluded several marketplace functional trainers that appear in many online
            roundups, because their own listings and the roundups gave different stack weights for
            the same model, and we could not find a maker specification to settle it. We also
            left out machines we could only find as discontinued or renamed listings.
          </p>
