        <div id="pulley-ratio" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Pulley Ratio: What 2:1 and 1:1 Mean in Plain Terms</h2>
<p>
            The pulley ratio is the single most misunderstood number on a cable machine, and it
            decides what the weight stack actually feels like in your hands.
          </p>
          <p>
            On a <strong>1:1</strong> machine, the cable runs from the handle over the pulleys
            straight to the stack. Pull the handle one foot and the stack rises one foot. What you
            select is what you lift: 100 pounds on the pin feels like 100 pounds at the handle.
          </p>
          <p>
            On a <strong>2:1</strong> machine, the cable is routed round a moving pulley so the
            effort is shared, a bit like a block and tackle. A 2:1 ratio halves the felt
            resistance: 100 pounds on the pin feels like roughly 50 pounds at the handle. In
            exchange, the handle travels twice as far as the stack moves, which is why 2:1 machines
            can offer long cable travel from a compact frame, and why they suit fast, full-range
            movements such as woodchops, lateral raises and single-arm presses.
          </p>
          <p>
            That has three practical consequences.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Halve the stack to know your top weight.</strong> A functional trainer
            with two 170 lb stacks at 2:1, like the Arcadia, gives you about 85 pounds of felt
            resistance per side. That is plenty for most single-arm and accessory work and may not
            be enough for heavy lat pulldowns or rows.</li>
            <li><strong>2:1 gives finer steps.</strong> A 10 lb plate on a 2:1 stack changes the
            felt load by about 5 pounds, which is genuinely useful for shoulders and arms.</li>
            <li><strong>Joining two cables gives you 1:1.</strong> Several machines, including
            both Bells of Steel units here, let you link the two cable ends or use centre pulleys
            to get a 1:1 pull for heavy pulldowns and rows. You give up cable travel and gain
            load.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Listings write ratios inconsistently. Some write 1:2 or 1/2:1 for what others call 2:1,
            and a few say "dual ratio" without explaining which pulley does what. The reliable test
            is the maker's own wording about felt weight: REP, for example, says that on its 2:1
            machines moving 100 pounds feels like 50. If a listing will not tell you what the
            stack feels like at the handle, assume nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            Digital machines such as the Tonal 2 and Speediance Gym Monster 2 do not have a stack
            or a pulley ratio in this sense. The figure they quote is the resistance the motors
            produce at the handles, so it compares with felt weight on an iron machine, not with
            stack weight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Which Type of Cable Machine Suits Your Space</h2>
<p>
            The five types on this page solve different problems, and the right one usually
            follows from what you already own and how much floor you can give up.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Functional trainer (dual weight stack)</h3>
          <p>
            Two columns, each with its own stack and an adjustable pulley carriage, joined by a
            frame and usually a <a href="/best-pull-up-bars/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>pull-up bar</a>. It is the most versatile type and the easiest to live
            with, because weight changes are a pin and height changes are a pop-pin. The cost is
            floor space: even a compact unit like the Arcadia is more than four and a half feet
            wide at the front. Choose this if the cable machine is the main event.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Rack-mounted cable attachment</h3>
          <p>
            A stack and pulley system that bolts into the uprights of a power rack, so the cables
            live inside a footprint you have already given up. It is the most space-efficient
            option and often cheaper than a standalone trainer, but it only fits specific racks,
            and the rack has to be anchored as its maker specifies. Choose this if you already own
            a compatible rack, and check which racks have attachment systems worth buying into
            before you choose one.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Plate-loaded pulley tower</h3>
          <p>
            A single upright with a pulley carriage and a loading pin or sleeve for your own
            plates. It is the cheapest way to get a proper adjustable cable, and the load is
            limited only by the cable's rating and your plates. The trade-offs are slower weight
            changes, one handle at a time, and a tall, narrow frame that has to be bolted to
            something. Choose this on a tight budget if you already own plates.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Compact all-in-one</h3>
          <p>
            A frame that combines cable columns with rack uprights, so one footprint covers
            pulldowns, rows, crossovers and, with J-cups and safety arms, barbell work. It saves
            space against a rack plus a trainer, but every compromise of both shows up somewhere:
            narrower uprights, a shorter lat bar, movements that are cramped. Choose this if you
            want one large machine instead of two. If you would rather have fixed press, leg and
            pulldown stations on a single weight stack than rack uprights, that is a different
            machine, compared in our guide to
            <a href="/best-home-gym-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">the best home gym machines</a>.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Connected digital-resistance machine</h3>
          <p>
            Motors provide the resistance, a screen runs the training, and there is no iron at
            all. These are the most compact and the quietest to adjust, and they can do things a
            stack cannot, such as adding load on the lowering phase. The costs are a lower maximum
            load than iron machines, dependence on software and power, and, for most, a paid
            membership or subscription for some or all features. Choose this if guided training
            matters more to you than maximum weight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space-and-ceiling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Footprint, Ceiling Height and Cable Travel</h2>
<p>
            Three measurements decide whether a cable machine fits, and the listed footprint is
            only one of them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Ceiling height.</strong> Most functional trainers and towers stand somewhere
            around six and a half to seven feet tall before you add flooring. Measure your ceiling
            at the exact spot the machine will go — basements often have beams, ducts or a low
            soffit exactly where you want the machine — and subtract the thickness of any <a href="/best-gym-flooring/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>rubber
            flooring</a> or platform, which raises the machine. Then add room above the frame for
            assembly: tall uprights are usually stood up from lying flat, and a frame that is only
            just shorter than the ceiling may not tip up into place. Several makers, including Bells
            of Steel, state an assembly clearance that is higher than the machine itself; check it
            on the product page. If you also want pull-ups on the built-in bar, you need clearance
            for your head above the bar, too.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Working space, not footprint.</strong> The base dimensions tell you what the
            frame covers. Cable exercises happen in front of it, and often to the side: a standing
            cable row or a crossover needs several feet of clear floor in front of the machine,
            and a woodchop needs room to rotate. Tonal recommends a 7 x 7-foot floor area in front
            of its unit, which is a sensible rule of thumb for any cable machine. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">space requirements for treadmills</a>
            uses the same approach — machine footprint plus a use zone plus a safety margin — and
            the method transfers directly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cable travel.</strong> This is how far the handle can be pulled from the
            pulley. Short travel limits tall users on overhead movements, long-stride lunges and
            full-range rows. A 2:1 ratio roughly doubles travel compared with 1:1 on the same
            frame, which is one reason most home trainers use it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Width between the uprights matters on functional trainers and all-in-ones. A narrow
            gap makes crossovers and some pressing movements feel cramped, and Garage Gym Reviews
            lists the Arcadia's usable width between uprights as a drawback. If you are broad or
            tall, check the internal width as well as the outside dimensions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="anchoring-and-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Anchoring, Wall Mounting and Safe Use</h2>
<p>
            A cable machine pulls sideways and upwards on its own frame every rep, and a tall,
            narrow frame is easier to tip than it looks. How each type is secured is part of the
            purchase, not an afterthought.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Bolt down or anchor what the maker says to.</strong> Bells of Steel says
            the plate-loaded All-in-One Trainer must be bolted down, and that both versions must be
            bolted down for barbell work; it recommends bolting its Cable Tower to a wall, the floor
            or a rack. Neither ships with the anchors. If you cannot drill your floor, many owners
            build a timber platform and bolt the machine to that — see our guide to
            <a href="/best-lifting-platforms/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lifting platforms</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Rack attachments are only as secure as the rack.</strong> A rack-mounted
            stack adds weight and leverage to one side of the rack. Anchor the rack as its maker
            specifies before adding one.</li>
            <li><strong>Wall-mounted digital machines need the right wall.</strong> The Tonal 2 is
            installed by Tonal's own installers onto wall studs or solid concrete, much like a
            heavy TV bracket, and needs a grounded outlet nearby. If your wall is unusual — old
            plaster, a stud layout you are unsure of, a partition wall — ask before you buy, and do
            not try to hang one yourself on drywall alone.</li>
            <li><strong>Upstairs and heavy machines.</strong> A dual-stack trainer with its stacks
            plus a pile of plates puts a lot of weight on a small area. On a ground-floor slab that
            is rarely a concern; on a suspended timber floor, place the machine near a load-bearing
            wall and across the joists rather than in the middle of a span, and consult a
            structural engineer for an older house or anything unusual.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            In use, the habits that prevent most cable injuries are simple. Check cables for
            fraying, kinks or flattened spots before you load the stack, and stop using a cable that
            shows any of them — on most machines cables and pulleys are consumables with a much
            shorter warranty than the frame. Keep fingers clear of the stack and the pulley
            carriage while adjusting. Confirm the selector pin or pop-pin is fully seated before
            you pull. Lower the stack under control rather than letting it crash, which is hard on
            the cables as well as noisy. Keep children away from stacks and carriages, and on
            digital machines use the maker's child lock or power-down routine. Treat every rated
            capacity on this page as a manufacturer figure, and stay well inside it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="attachments" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Attachments: What Comes in the Box and What to Add</h2>
<p>
            Most cable machines come with very little. REP says the Arcadia ships with D-handles
            and the Athena with a urethane D-handle, and Bells of Steel lists adjustable nylon
            handles, carabiners and its 2-to-1 adapter with the stack version of its Cable Tower. That is enough to start, but most owners add a few
            attachments in the first month.
          </p>
          <p>
            The ones worth buying first, in rough order of usefulness:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Tricep rope.</strong> Pushdowns, face pulls, overhead extensions and
            cable crunches. Cheap and used constantly.</li>
            <li><strong>Lat pulldown bar.</strong> Check whether one is included; the Bells of
            Steel All-in-One Trainer has its own shortened bar that fits between its uprights,
            and a standard-length bar will not.</li>
            <li><strong>Close-grip row handle (V or double-D).</strong> Seated and standing rows,
            and close-grip pulldowns.</li>
            <li><strong>Ankle straps.</strong> Kickbacks, hip abduction and leg curls.</li>
            <li><strong>Straight or EZ bar.</strong> Curls, upright rows and pressdowns with both
            hands.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Most home cable machines use standard carabiner connections, so third-party
            attachments generally fit, but check the carabiner opening and the width of any bar
            against the gap between uprights. Digital machines are different: Tonal sells its own
            accessory bundles, priced separately from the unit, that are required to unlock many
            movements, and Speediance sells its machine in packages with different accessories.
            Price the accessories you need before comparing a digital machine with an iron one.
            Our guide to
            <a href="/best-home-gym-accessories/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home gym accessories</a>
            covers storage for all of it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reading-listings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Reading a Cable Machine Listing</h2>
<p>
            Cable machine listings are full of numbers that sound comparable and are not. Five
            checks will catch most of the traps.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Stack weight or felt weight?</strong> A listing that says "340 lb of
            resistance" may mean two 170 lb stacks at 2:1, which is about 85 pounds per handle.
            Find the ratio, then halve if it is 2:1.</li>
            <li><strong>Is the "max capacity" a cable rating or a stack?</strong> On plate-loaded
            and band-assisted machines, the headline figure is often the most the cable is rated
            to carry, not a weight that comes with the machine.</li>
            <li><strong>Does the price include the stack?</strong> Some trainers are sold with
            stacks, some without, and some with a smaller stack and an upgrade kit.</li>
            <li><strong>What mounting does it need, and is the hardware included?</strong> If the
            listing says "must be bolted" or "wall-mounted", find out what to and with what.</li>
            <li><strong>Which rack does it fit?</strong> Rack attachments are usually specific to
            one maker's racks, hole size and upright dimensions, and sometimes to one generation
            of a rack.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Be particularly careful with marketplace functional trainers and multi-gyms that
            appear in long online roundups. In our checks, the same model was described with
            different stack weights in different places. When figures disagree and the maker does
            not publish a spec sheet, treat every number with suspicion.
          </p>
          <p>
            For digital machines, read the membership terms as closely as the hardware spec. Tonal
            states that each purchase requires a 12-month membership commitment and that an active
            membership is needed for most features. Speediance offers an optional subscription,
            and PCWorld's review notes that some functions require it. In both cases, check the
            current price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If you mainly want heavy compound lifts</strong>, a rack, a barbell and plates
            will do more for the money than any cable machine, and you can add a rack-mounted cable
            attachment later. Most home cable machines top out at a felt load that is fine for
            accessories and modest for heavy pulling.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If your budget is under a few hundred dollars</strong>, <a href="/best-resistance-bands/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>resistance bands</a> and a
            door anchor cover many cable movements for a fraction of the cost, and adjustable
            dumbbells cover most of the rest. Our guide to
            <a href="/best-adjustable-dumbbells/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">adjustable dumbbells</a>
            is the better starting point.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you need a commercial-duty machine</strong> for a studio or a shared
            building gym, look at commercial lines with longer cable warranties and service
            networks. Our guide to
            <a href="/commercial-gym-equipment/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial gym equipment</a>
            explains what changes at that level.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If cardio is the priority</strong>, a cable machine will not replace it. Many
            home gyms pair one strength station with one cardio machine, and our
            <a href="/home-cardio-equipment-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home cardio equipment guide</a>
            helps with that side of the decision, including whether a treadmill, bike or rower
            earns the floor space next to your cable machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best cable machine for a home gym?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people with the space, a dual-stack functional trainer such as the REP Arcadia, because weight and height changes are quick and it covers almost every cable movement. If you already own a compatible rack, a rack-mounted attachment saves floor space. On a tight budget with plates already owned, a plate-loaded tower is the cheapest proper option.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does a 2:1 pulley ratio mean on a cable machine?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 2:1 ratio halves the felt resistance. With 100 pounds selected on the stack, you feel roughly 50 pounds at the handle, and the handle travels about twice as far as the stack moves. A 1:1 machine gives you the full selected weight with shorter cable travel. Always halve a 2:1 stack to know your real top load.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much ceiling height do I need for a cable machine?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Measure first. Many functional trainers and towers stand somewhere around six and a half to seven feet tall, flooring raises them further, and assembly often needs extra clearance above the frame. Tonal states that its wall unit needs 7 feet 10 inches of ceiling for the arms to extend fully. Check each maker's stated height and assembly clearance.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do cable machines need to be bolted down?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many do. Bells of Steel says its plate-loaded All-in-One Trainer must be bolted down and recommends anchoring its Cable Tower to a wall, floor or rack. Tall, narrow frames can tip under sideways pulls. Follow the maker's instructions exactly, and if you cannot drill the floor, a bolted timber platform is a common alternative.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a functional trainer better than a rack-mounted cable attachment?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A standalone functional trainer is more versatile and works anywhere, but needs its own floor space. A rack attachment fits inside the rack footprint and usually costs less, but only fits specific racks, and the rack must be anchored. If you already own a compatible rack, the attachment is usually the better use of space.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do Tonal and Speediance need a subscription?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tonal requires a 12-month membership commitment with each purchase, and an active membership is needed for most features. Speediance sells an optional subscription; reviewers report the machine works without it for basic strength training but some functions need it. Prices change, so check the current membership price before buying either machine.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Home Cable Machine</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these turns an expensive, heavy purchase into one that disappoints or does not fit.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading stack weight as felt weight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Two 170 lb stacks at 2:1 give about 85 pounds per handle, not 340. Buyers who plan heavy pulldowns and rows on a 2:1 machine often find it runs out early. Check the ratio, halve a 2:1 stack, and if heavy pulling matters, choose a machine with a 1:1 option or keep a barbell for those lifts.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Measuring the floor but not the ceiling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A machine that fits the floor can still fail at the ceiling: flooring raises it, beams and ducts sit exactly where you want it, and tall uprights need extra clearance to stand up during assembly. Measure at the exact spot, subtract flooring thickness, and check the stated assembly clearance as well as the frame height.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the anchoring</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Tall, narrow cable frames are pulled sideways every rep, and several makers state they must be bolted to the floor, a wall or a rack, with hardware not included. Treat anchors as part of the purchase. If you cannot drill, plan a bolted platform before delivery rather than training on an unsecured frame.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pricing a digital machine on hardware alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The screen price is not the cost of ownership. Tonal requires a membership commitment and sells accessories separately; Speediance sells an optional subscription and bundles with different accessories. Add the membership, accessories and installation to the hardware price, then compare the total with an iron machine plus the attachments you would add to it.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

